rep('07-services-queue-scheduler.md', [
("## 7. Services: Queue Worker, Scheduler and Tuning", "## 7. Services: queue worker, scheduler and tuning"),
("Imports, EIR calculations, revenue runs and LGD payment tracking run on the database queue and need a worker that is always up.",
 "Imports, EIR calculations, revenue runs started from a screen, discounting and LGD payment tracking run on the database queue (`QUEUE_CONNECTION=database`, the `default` queue) and need a worker that is always up. The bootstrap and the Maintenance jobs (chapter 7.2) do not use the queue: the bootstrap runs everything synchronously, and the Maintenance screen starts each job as a detached console process."),
("`--timeout=7200` matches the longest job (`ProcessLGDPayments`); `--max-time=3600` makes the worker exit and restart hourly so it always runs current code. After every deployment run `php artisan queue:restart`, which `scripts/deploy.sh` already does. One worker is enough for MAIIC's volumes; add a second unit if two long imports must overlap.",
 "`--timeout=7200` matches the longest job (`ProcessLGDPayments`, 7,200 seconds; `CalculateDiscountingJob` allows 3,600, the EIR import, calculation and revenue jobs 600); `--max-time=3600` makes the worker exit and restart once it has run for an hour, after the job in hand, so it picks up new code. After every deployment run `php artisan queue:restart`, which `scripts/deploy.sh` already does.\n\nRun one worker only. `config/queue.php` gives the `database` connection `retry_after` of 90 seconds, so with a second worker any job still running after 90 seconds would be taken again by the other worker and run twice. A second unit is safe only after `retry_after` has been raised above the longest job's timeout in a release."),
("Today the schedule polls the E-Banker feed folder daily at 06:30 (`eir:poll-feed-folder --also-route-3`), imports the World Bank macro series on the 2nd of each month at 07:00 (`macro:import-worldbank`), and runs the legacy campaign and reminder commands. The month-end chain (staging, PD, the forward-looking adjustment, ECL and the EIR revenue run) is started from Administration, Maintenance (`maiic:task month-end`) or by hand (Technical Manual chapter 9).",
 "`app/Console/Kernel.php` schedules:\n\n| When | Command | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Daily at 06:30, never two at once | `eir:poll-feed-folder --also-route-3` | Lands every new E-Banker pack in `EBANKER_FEED_FOLDER` (route 2) and in the inbox the API fills (route 3, `storage/app/ebanker-inbox`) through the gates; with no folder set it warns and lands nothing |\n| The 2nd of each month at 07:00 | `macro:import-worldbank` | Fetches the World Bank series live; a series it cannot reach comes from the committed snapshot in `docs/bootstrap/macro` |\n| Daily, every five minutes, every two minutes | `campaigns:process-recurring`, `campaigns:process-scheduled`, `reminders:loan-applications` | Legacy commands of the credit scoring platform; they find nothing to do on a MAIIC database |\n\n`php artisan schedule:list` shows the same list with the next run times.\n\nThe month-end chain is not scheduled. It is started from Administration & support, Support & Maintenance, Maintenance, Month-end tab (\"Run the month-end calculations\", which runs `maiic:task month-end`) or by hand (chapter 9.9 and Technical Manual chapter 9). The Maintenance screen runs one job at a time as a detached process of the web server user with `php -d memory_limit=4096M`, and keeps each job's output under `storage/app/command-runs`; that user must be able to write there and run `php`."),
("Apply the values in chapter 5.3. With 32 GB of memory the default PHP-FPM pool (`pm = dynamic`, `pm.max_children = 20`) is adequate; raise `pm.max_children` only if the access log shows queued requests.",
 "Apply the values in chapter 5.3. With 32 GB of memory the default PHP-FPM pool (`pm = dynamic`, `pm.max_children = 20`) is adequate; raise `pm.max_children` only if the access log shows queued requests. Bear in mind that the screens which read the whole E-Banker ledger raise their own request to 4 GB (chapter 6.1), so a few of them open at once, beside a running month-end job, can use a large share of the server's memory."),
("Use a quarter to a half of available memory for the buffer pool on a dedicated database host; 2 GB is ample for the current database.",
 "Use a quarter to a half of available memory for the buffer pool on a dedicated database host; 2 GB is ample for the current database (about 1 GB after the bootstrap)."),
])
